        "subtitles": [
          {
            "text": "You've typed a function parameter as `any` just to get your code compiling, haven't you?",
            "start": 0.1,
            "end": 5.212,
            "startMs": 100,
            "endMs": 5212,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "We've all done it.",
            "start": 5.162,
            "end": 6.95,
            "startMs": 5162,
            "endMs": 6950,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "But the second you use `any`, you strip away every single safety guarantee TypeScript gives you.",
            "start": 6.95,
            "end": 12.963,
            "startMs": 6950,
            "endMs": 12962,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "You lose autocomplete, you lose compile-time checking, and you invite runtime crashes into production.",
            "start": 12.963,
            "end": 19.7,
            "startMs": 12962,
            "endMs": 19700,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "Generics exist to solve this exact dilemma: they give you ultimate flexibility without throwing away type safety.",
            "start": 19.7,
            "end": 26.862,
            "startMs": 19700,
            "endMs": 26862,
            "type": "sentence"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "section_2",
        "narration": "Think of a generic as a parameter, but instead of passing a value like a string or number, you're passing a type. When you write a generic function, TypeScript looks at the real runtime argument and automatically captures its exact shape on the fly. <break time='0.6s'/> Look at the difference. Without generics, using `any` completely destroys your return type tracking. The editor has no idea what comes out. But add `<T>` to the signature, and TypeScript dynamically binds `T` to whatever enters the function. If you pass an array of numbers, `T` becomes `number`. Return `T`, and your editor immediately knows every method available on that return value. Total safety, zero duplication.",
        "objective": "",
        "visuals": [
          {
