      {
        "id": "section_3_v0",
        "type": "CodeEditorScene",
        "title": "Generic Constraints",
        "subtitle": "Restricting generics with extends",
        "narration": "Generics get absurdly powerful once you add constraints using the `extends` keyword. Unconstrained generics accept literally anything — numbers, booleans, null. But what if your generic function needs to access a `.length` property, or pick a key from an object? <break time='0.6s'/> By writing `T extends { length: number }`, you enforce that `T` must have a length, while still preserving its exact shape. Take it further with the `keyof` operator. You can make a second type parameter, `K`, extend `keyof T`. Suddenly, you've built a function that guarantees at compile-time that a property exists on an object. Pass an invalid key? TypeScript catches it instantly before you even hit save.",
        "actualDurationSec": 0,
        "estimatedDurationSec": 0,
        "payload": {
          "engine": "",
          "inputs": {},
          "layout": "fullscreen",
          "code": "interface HasLength {\n  length: number;\n}\n\nfunction logLength<T extends HasLength>(item: T): T {\n  console.log(item.length);\n  return item;\n}\n\nlogLength('hello');   // OK\nlogLength([1, 2, 3]); // OK\nlogLength(123);       // Error: number has no .length",
          "language": "typescript",
          "filename": "constraints.ts",
          "highlightLines": [
            5,
            12
          ],
          "callout": "Extends acts as a guard, requiring specific properties.",
